== Which models are there? ==
The list changes often, so treat it as a snapshot and check the model picker in the app.

{| class="wikitable sortable"
! Model !! Access !! Notes
|-
| DeepSeek V4.1 Flash || Free and paid || Fast coding and tool use, 1M context
|-
| DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Slow || Free and paid || Same model, streamed slower; the default on limited access without a plan, and in Chat
|-
| Solar Mini 4 || Free and paid || Upstage, compact, 500K context, text only
|-
| Solar Pro 4 || Free and paid || Upstage, larger, 500K context, text only
|-
| Muse Spark 1.3 || Paid || Meta agentic coding model, 1M context
|-
| Muse Ember || Paid || Meta fast coding model, 375K context
|-
| Claude Haiku 5.5 || Paid || Fast Anthropic model, 100K context
|-
| GPT-6.1 Terra || Paid || OpenAI, two sessions a day, 1M context, images
|-
| Beam 1.0 · Glyph Cluster · Nemotron 3.5 Lightning || Free experimental || Limited-access tiers
|}

* Each model costs hours at the rate shown in the picker: a one-hour model uses one hour of balance, a three-hour model uses three, and free experimental models use none.
* Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite does specialist jobs such as file finding, rather than appearing in the main picker.
* Retired already: GLM 5.3 Flash, MiMo 2.6 Pro, GPT-6.1 Sol, Gemini 4 Argon, Gemini 3.8 Flash.
* Desktop can also run locally installed Claude Code and Codex agents on your own provider account; those are separate from this catalog.
* On phones the model picker is gone on purpose: mobile uses DeepSeek only, for a simpler screen.
* On data: a model may only be used for training when its notice says so. Separately, usage data may train the ad-selection models; project files and connected repositories are not given to advertising providers.

== What do the plans include? ==
{| class="wikitable sortable"
! Plan !! Full access !! Limited access !! Bonus (full / limited)
|-
| Starter || 50 || 21 || +30 / +20
|-
| Plus || 80 || 40 || +50 / +40
|-
| Pro || 130 || 70 || +100 / +80
|}

* Limited access still works: it is a smaller allowance, not a block. Paid allowances follow the same full-or-limited split, not the country list.
* The daily pool is spent before your wallet. It refills at midnight in your account's reset timezone, and unused hours do not carry over.
* After 7 days in a row, eligible days add 3 hours to the daily pool. The bonus lands on your first message that day and expires at the reset if you do not use it.
* Plans bought before 9 November 2026 keep their old bonus while the subscription continues. Upgrading keeps the current bonus and adds the new plan's bonus. Cancelling a monthly plan ends its benefits immediately; cancelling an annual plan stops renewal but access continues for the paid year.
* Referral and bounty hours go into a wallet and are spent after the daily allowance.

== What do the words mean? ==
; Agent
: The AI that does the work, not just answers. Here its name is Buffy.
; Model
: The part that writes the text and picks the next action; different models cost different amounts of hours.
; Tool
: One concrete action the agent can take: read a file, search text, run a command.
; Runtime
: Where the work happens — your computer or a cloud workspace.
; Prompt
: The message you type to ask for something.
; Repo (repository)
: A project folder tracked by git, so changes can be reviewed or undone.
; Terminal
: The text window where commands are typed. Freebuff can use it for you.
; Build
: Turning source code into something that runs, and checking that it does.
; Sandbox / workspace
: A separate place where the project runs. Cloud has one; Desktop does not isolate your OS.
; Mode
: Plan (read only) or Build (can change files).
; Subagent
: A helper agent with one narrow job; its result goes back to the main agent.
; Base3
: The name of Freebuff's main loop: one agent investigates, edits and verifies with its own tools.
; Freebucks
: The older credit unit; balances are in hours now, and 1 hour = 5 Freebucks.
; Referral link
: A link that credits whoever shared it when a new user signs up.

